      <section class="border-bottom-new-design py-5">
        <div class="container">
          <h2 class="text-center mb-3">Types of crypto wallets</h2>
          <p class="text-center mb-5">Selecting the right DeFi wallet has become increasingly difficult
            for users as the number of wallets increases, making it crucial that users carefully consider their
            options. There are two types of crypto wallets, known as “hot” and “cold” wallets, which offer different
            levels of utility and security.</p>
          <div class="row row-cols-1 row-cols-lg-2 g-4">
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card hover-card-border-style h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2"> Hot wallets</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">They are managed online. They offer both the convenience and
                    accessibility of internet-based financial solutions but also come with the usual risks associated
                    with internet access. Personal hot wallets are useful for basic transactions but are not the best
                    place for holding the bulk of your digital assets.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="col">
              <div class="card hover-card-border-style h-100">
                <div class="card-body">
                  <i class="fa-solid fa-layer-group fs-24 fc-b"></i>
                  <h5 class="card-title mt-2">Cold wallets</h5>
                  <p class="card-text">The most secure way to store digital assets. Cold wallets, also
                    known as hardware wallets, aren't connected to the internet and therefore can't be accessed as
                    easily. Bad actors would need physical possession of the device itself, along with PINs,
                    passwords, and other security measures to gain access to the assets inside. </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

